  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1220 PM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nWidespread showers and thunderstorms will likely be ongoing \nThursday morning, with the line/broken line gradually \ndeteriorating and sliding east of the forecast area by mid \nafternoon. As the upper low lifts northeastward into southwest \nKansas Thursday afternoon, the aforementioned mid-level dry slot \nwill move over eastern Oklahoma. Hi-res CAMs are in agreement \nthat the dry slot, along with an approaching cold front from the \nnorth, will contribute to destabilization and production of \nshowers and thunderstorms along and just ahead of the frontal \nboundary in eastern Oklahoma during the mid to late afternoon \ntimeframe. Severe, damaging wind gusts and heavy rain will be the \nprimary severe threats, but there might be enough dry air aloft \nand low- level wind shear for isolated marginally severe hail and \na quick spin up tornado or two, respectively. \n\nAs the upper low continues to lift northeastward into the \nCentral/Northern Plains Thursday evening into the overnight hours \nThursday night, it will help push a cold front into the forecast \narea. The heavy rainfall axis Thursday night into Friday morning \nlooks to set up south and east of I-44, ahead of the approaching \nfront. The front gradually pushes south of the area by late Friday\nafternoon/early evening, with precipitation chances decreasing \nfrom north to south behind it. The upper-level trough axis will \nstick around and hover over the area through at least Saturday, \nmaintaining low to medium rain (with isolated thunder) chances \n(20-50%). As the trough axis finally departs Saturday night into \nSunday morning, a weak upper-level low, centered over central \nTexas, may keep light shower chances across southeast Oklahoma \nthrough the day on Sunday, as well. Rainfall amounts stay light on\nSaturday and Sunday, especially compared to rainfall amounts over\nthe next couple of days. \n\nTotal rainfall amounts through the upcoming weekend could vary \ngreatly across the forecast area. In general, amounts 1 to 3 \ninches will be common for most locations. However, consistent \nbanding and training, along with the significantly heavier \ndownpours, will likely cause isolated footprints of 5 inches or \ngreater in spots. On the flip side, it is possible a few areas \nmay not even reach an inch of rain. The heaviest rainfall is still\ncurrently forecast to occur late tonight through Friday morning. \nWidespread nuisance/minor flooding is likely, with isolated flash \nflooding possible at times. The greater flooding potential remains \nover east central and southeast Oklahoma.\n\nBehind the cold front, temperatures are finally forecast to \nbecome and remain cooler than average through the majority of the \nlong-term. Anticipate daytime high temperatures ranging from the \nmid to upper 70s and overnight low temperatures generally from the\nlow to mid 50s to lower 60s through the rest of the week into \nnext week.\n\nMejia",
